[sedgyuedmonastery.org](https://sedgyuedmonastery.org/products/tsog-shing?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

A Gelug merit field typically arranges teachers, Buddhas, bodhisattvas, tantric deities and protectors in a structured assembly.

The Sanskrit puṇya means merit or the positive potential associated with wholesome action. Kṣetra means field.

In its simplest sense, a field is an object in relation to which an intentional action produces karmic results.

This is more technical than the agricultural metaphor initially suggests.

Imagine two actions:

- You offer food to an ordinary person.
- You offer food to a Buddha.

Suppose your generosity and the material gift are otherwise identical.

In the traditional Buddhist karmic account, these actions need not produce identical results. The qualities of the recipient are themselves causally significant.

The Buddha is a supreme field because his mental continuum is understood to be free of the obscurations that ordinarily condition suffering and ignorance.

But notice: the merit doesn't exist inside the Buddha as something transmitted to the donor.

The merit arises in dependence on the donor's intentional action toward that object.

The Buddha's qualities make the act a particularly powerful karmic cause.

### Tsongkhapa's actual causal analysis

In Lamrim Chenmo, Volume I, pp. 231–235, Tsongkhapa identifies four considerations affecting the power of an action:

| Factor           | Technical significance                                                              |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Recipient        | The qualities and relationship of the being toward whom the action is directed      |
| Support          | The ethical commitments and spiritual condition of the person performing the action |
| Object or action | What is actually offered or done                                                    |
| Attitude         | The motivation, intensity, and purpose behind the action                            |

He explicitly includes the Three Jewels, spiritual teachers and parents among especially consequential recipients. Even a seemingly small action toward them can have a powerful result.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.theosophy-ult.org.uk\&sz=32)

theosophy-ult.org.uk+1



Here is the essential principle: karma is relationally conditioned, rather than determined solely by outward behaviour.

The same visible movement of your hand can participate in very different causal processes depending on the object, intention and understanding involved.

This is why the quality of the field matters.

And Tsongkhapa goes further. On page 235 he cites a Mahāyāna scripture comparing enormous offerings with a bodhisattva's offering of a single flower, the latter being considered greater because of its orientation toward complete Buddhahood.

Thus, the field alone doesn't determine the result. The intention can radically transform the significance of the act.&#x20;

