## 2. What exactly is merit, metaphysically?

Here we reach a difficult problem.

If you offer a flower to a Buddha, the offering happens and ends. Your intention also arises and ceases.

Yet Buddhism maintains that the action may produce results years, lifetimes or even eons later.

What persists after the action has disappeared?

The standard answer involves karmic potentials (puṇya in the case of positive potential), which can give rise to results when appropriate conditions assemble.

But Tsongkhapa has a distinctive explanation of their ontological status.

### Merit is not a substance deposited in consciousness

Some Buddhist philosophical systems explain karmic continuity using ālayavijñāna, the storehouse consciousness, which carries karmic seeds.

Tsongkhapa's mature Prāsaṅgika Madhyamaka rejects the necessity of this foundational consciousness.

In the Gelug account, karmic potentials are dependently imputed upon the conventionally existent person, the mere I, which itself depends upon the aggregates and their continuity.

They aren't physically stored objects. Nor do they require an unchanging spiritual substance to preserve them.

Gelug scholasticism also employs the unusual concept of an action's having-disintegrated (zhig pa), conventionally establishing continuity between an action's cessation and its eventual result.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://studybuddhism.com\&sz=32)

Study Buddhism+1



Consider the implications.

When you perform an intentional action, it ceases, but its causal efficacy need not cease with it. The action establishes a dependent causal relation capable of yielding results under appropriate conditions.

That's why merit is better understood as positive karmic potential, not as an amount of spiritual energy contained somewhere in your body.

The claim that such potentials persist across lives is part of the traditional metaphysical system, not an independently demonstrated scientific account.

## 3. The metaphysical crux: why emptiness makes merit possible

This is arguably Tsongkhapa's deepest contribution.

His two truths are:

Conventional truth (saṃvṛti-satya): Actions, intentions, people, Buddhas, karmic causes and their results conventionally exist and function.

Ultimate truth (paramārtha-satya): None of these phenomena exists independently, through its own intrinsic nature (svabhāva).

Now imagine that karma possessed intrinsic existence.

Its nature would be self-established, independent of conditions. But an independently established thing could not genuinely be transformed by other causes.

Tsongkhapa argues that it is precisely because things are not self-established that causation and transformation are intelligible.

In his In Praise of Dependent Arising, he explains that dependent origination and emptiness are mutually compatible. Indeed, dependence demonstrates the absence of intrinsic existence.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://studybuddhism.com\&sz=32)

Study Buddhism



Apply this to the merit field:

The donor is empty. The Buddha is empty. The offering is empty. The intention is empty. And the resulting merit is empty.

Yet these conditions still function together.

We could express the structure schematically:

An intentional act

Arising through donor, recipient, motivation, action and circumstances

Dependently arisen karmic potential

Neither an independently existing substance nor a mere nothing

Ripening under further conditions

Wholesome results, favourable circumstances and conditions supportive of awakening

This is a conceptual reconstruction, not a numerical karmic formula.

The point is that there is no intrinsically existing merit to discover, but that does not mean there is no merit.

This is the Madhyamaka middle way between reification and nihilism.&#x20;

