In a Gelug guru-yoga practice called Ganden Lha Gyäma, Tsongkhapa is visualized as appearing from the Tuṣita pure realm, accompanied by his principal disciples.

The practitioner makes offerings, requests teachings and blessings, and cultivates confidence in the lineage.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.lamayeshe.com\&sz=32)

Lama Yeshe Wisdom Archive



This isn't supposed to mean that all the beings in the painting are literally sitting together somewhere in a giant celestial tree.

The painting is an intentionally constructed sacred assembly, bringing together beings associated with different times, lineages and states of awakening.

But traditionally, Buddhas and bodhisattvas are not regarded as merely imaginary characters. Their compassionate activity is understood to extend beyond ordinary physical limitations.

The important distinction is that the appearance you visualize and the being you take as its referent are not simply identical.

One is a meditative representation. The other, within the tradition, is an awakened being or spiritual source of refuge.

### Does the Buddha have to be watching you?

Not necessarily. The merit-generating function of an offering doesn't require imagining a Buddha consciously accepting a transaction.

Tsongkhapa explicitly emphasizes offerings to the Three Jewels, and allows offerings made through visualized mandalas and simple substances, such as water, when there are no material possessions available.

In Lamrim Chenmo, Volume I, pp. 200–201, he stresses the importance of faith and intention in making offerings.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.theosophy-ult.org.uk\&sz=32)

theosophy-ult.org.uk



Thus, the practice can function as karmic cultivation without any need to posit a Buddha individually processing each offering.

The additional Vajrayāna claim is that enlightened beings can also provide byin rlabs—usually translated as blessing or inspiration.

In Gelug practice, this means an influence conducive to spiritual realization, especially through the relationship with the spiritual teacher and lineage. It should not be confused with the claim that merit itself is a transferable substance.

## 5. The field has two functions: merit and wisdom

Here's where the metaphysics becomes especially elegant.

Tsongkhapa does not teach that accumulating merit by itself constitutes enlightenment.

The Mahāyāna path requires two accumulations, saṃbhāradvaya:

| Accumulation            | Primary function                                                                          | Buddhahood result                                                |
| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Puṇya-saṃbhāra — merit  | Develops generosity, ethics, patience, compassionate activity and other aspects of method | Rūpakāya, the form bodies through which a Buddha benefits beings |
| Jñāna-saṃbhāra — wisdom | Develops realization of emptiness, overcoming ignorance and its obscurations              | Dharmakāya, the truth body, including awakened wisdom            |

These are interdependent, not two entirely independent projects.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.lamayeshe.com\&sz=32)

Lama Yeshe Wisdom Archive+1



And now you can see why a field of merit might be treated as much more than a devotional exercise.

Merit provides conditions favourable to developing wisdom, while wisdom changes the way meritorious acts are understood and performed.

Without merit, the practitioner may lack the supportive conditions for sustained study, concentration and insight.

Without wisdom, even enormous quantities of virtuous karma do not by themselves eliminate ignorance.

In Lamrim Chenmo, Tsongkhapa treats accumulation and purification as supporting conditions for realizations. He explicitly prescribes the visualization of a field containing lineage masters, Buddhas, bodhisattvas, and other realized beings in the preparatory phase of meditation (Volume I, p. 94).&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.theosophy-ult.org.uk\&sz=32)

theosophy-ult.org.uk



This is why an academically oriented practitioner in the Gelug tradition may spend considerable time making offerings and prostrations before studying emptiness.

These practices are understood to help establish the conditions for insight, not replace the reasoning through which insight is developed.

## 6. How the practice works in real time

Imagine sitting before one of those elaborate thangkas.

Rather than merely admiring the artwork, you visualize the complete assembly of teachers and enlightened beings in the space before you.

You then perform the seven-limb practice (saptāṅga-pūjā).

1

Homage

You acknowledge the qualities of awakened beings, counteracting pride.

2

Offerings

You cultivate generosity and release attachment.

3

Confession

You acknowledge harmful actions, develop regret and undertake purification.

4

Rejoicing

You cultivate delight in the virtue of others, opposing envy.

5

Requesting teachings

You cultivate receptivity to Dharma and its transmission.

6

Requesting the teachers to remain

You express the wish for the continued availability of the teachings.

7

Dedication (pariṇāmanā)

You dedicate the wholesome potential to complete awakening for the benefit of all beings.

This is how the field is cultivated: distinct intentions and acts are repeatedly generated in relation to the assembly.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://thubtenchodron.org\&sz=32)

Thubten Chodron+1



The field is therefore not passive. It becomes the intentional object through which a coordinated transformation of attitudes is practised.

The fascinating part is that even rejoicing in somebody else's virtue becomes a meritorious act, without needing to possess what that person possesses or perform the same action yourself.

That illustrates how central intentional participation is to the theory.

## 7. The highest level: the emptiness of the three spheres

There is an even subtler level to all this.

Ordinary generosity may implicitly involve three apparently solid entities:

The giver, the gift, and the recipient.

But wisdom realizing emptiness understands that none of the three possesses independent existence.

This is the emptiness of the three spheres (trimaṇḍala-pariśuddhi).

Suppose you're making a flower offering.

Conventionally, you're offering a flower to a Buddha. That action remains meaningful.

Ultimately, however, there is no intrinsically existent giver, no intrinsically existent gift and no intrinsically existent recipient.

Perfecting generosity involves the union of compassionate action and the wisdom that understands its emptiness.

This doesn't invalidate the action. It purifies the mistaken way in which the action is apprehended.

In Mahāyāna scholasticism, that union becomes essential to the full development of the perfections.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.encyclopediaofbuddhism.org\&sz=32)

Encyclopedia of Buddhism+1



Notice how different this is from merely trying to collect an enormous amount of positive karma.

A person could accumulate merit while still clinging to an intrinsically existent self. Such merit may produce fortunate experiences without uprooting the ignorance responsible for cyclic existence.

The bodhisattva's project is to bring merit together with the wisdom that eventually eliminates that ignorance.

## 8. The paradox: a Buddha is empty, but can still help you

We can now answer your question about deathless bodhisattvas much more precisely.

In Tsongkhapa's system, awakening doesn't produce an eternal soul existing independently in another realm.

A Buddha is empty of intrinsic existence just as an ordinary sentient being is.

But the Buddha is understood to have eliminated the obscurations that limit wisdom and compassionate activity.

The distinction is therefore not between an intrinsically existent supernatural being and an ordinary unreal human.

It is between different dependently established states of mind and different capacities for benefiting beings.

The fully awakened Buddha possesses the dharmakāya and can manifest form bodies (rūpakāya). Mahāyāna Buddhism understands these manifestations as ways Buddhas continue to teach and help beings, without being subjected to ordinary samsaric rebirth.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://bodhicitta.tsadra.org\&sz=32)

Bodhicitta+1



So, within that religious framework, the answer is indeed that enlightened beings are continuously capable of helping.

But the merit field is not a spatial map showing all their present addresses. It is the contemplative organization of those beings and their qualities as objects of practice.

This also helps explain why the root spiritual teacher has such an important position in Gelug merit-field practice.

The teacher links the practitioner to an actual lineage of instruction. Their role is not merely to embody an attractive ideal, but to provide the conditions under which the teachings can be understood and realized.

In the Ganden Lha Gyäma commentary, Lama Zopa Rinpoche describes the spiritual teacher as an exceptionally powerful merit field, and emphasizes that putting the teacher's genuine Dharma instructions into practice is superior to material offerings alone.&#x20;

