## 9. One final subtlety: why the merit field isn't just psychological

A purely psychological interpretation would say:

You contemplate compassion, generosity and wisdom, thereby gradually reinforcing those mental qualities.

That's a meaningful part of the practice.

But it is not the whole traditional account.

The classical account also asserts karmic results extending beyond observable psychological changes, including across lifetimes, and the continuing enlightened activity of Buddhas and bodhisattvas.

These are metaphysical commitments that cannot simply be reduced to attention training without changing the doctrine.

Equally, Tsongkhapa doesn't permit us to treat Buddhas as inherently existing divine substances from which blessings automatically emanate.

His Madhyamaka requires that even the Buddha, the teacher, the blessing and its recipient exist dependently.

This gives the practice a distinctive logic: there is genuine causal efficacy without an independently existing source of that efficacy.

